# local env
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Deployment env files. These live in a checkout on each EC2 host and are edited
# in place there, so an operator running `git add -A` while troubleshooting would
# otherwise commit real production credentials - into a public repository. The
# runbooks say "never commit .env.prod"; this is what enforces it.
#
# Ignore every .env.<environment> and re-admit the committed templates, rather
# than listing prod and qa by name, so a third environment is covered the day it
# exists instead of the day someone remembers to add it.
.env.*
!.env.*.example

